Date: Mon, 7 Jun 93 19:36:56 -0400
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: BRAIN: obscure speculation

I'm very happy to see that my speculations have been received with such
intelligent contributions and criticisms...  So I'll continue...

Nick Szabo writes:

>The basis of biological homeostasis is
>metabolism, the network of chemical reactions that keeps us
>alive and serves to propagate our genes. I suggest we can take this 
>view further by determining how nervous systems interacts with 
>metabolisms.

Yes, and interaction with metabolisms is a major function of the nervous system
(I use the term function to mean "what the thing accomplishes", not necessarily
implying intentional behavior - more below).  Perhaps the methods by which the
nervous system maintains metabolic states are analogous to the methods by which
it orients within the environment?  Consider the Extended Phenotype notion
that behavior and morphology are essentially equivalent from a
gene-centered viewpoint.  The extension of homeostatic behavior into the
environment external to the organism would not require a major evolutionary
innovation, it simply requires the complexification of an interface with the
environment.

On a similar note, most people consider the "higher" brain functions (like
cognition) to have evolved last, and to be epitomized by humans.  This is
supported by the notion that the high-level stuff goes on in the cerebral
cortex which was the last to evolve among brain structures.  However, there is
increasing evidence and opinion that subcortical structures are the actual
substrate for much of what we call cognition and that the cerebrum and
cerebellum "merely" provide support, or side paths.  In human development
(ontogeny follows phylogeny!) it appears that the cerebral cortex takes over
in adults some of the functionality implemented in infants by the subcortical
structures.  Other researchers suggest that the critical differences of
homans over lower mammals are in more sophisticated perceptual processing.
This makes perfect sense, IMHO, as even the simplest creatures needed to be a
complete system, requiring cognition, perception, and action.  What I'm trying
to speculate about is the basic function of a system that later diversified
into cognitive, perceptual, and action subsystems...

>We can relate metabolism to the computational/informational model
>by give metabolites themselves an information value.  It's easy
>to measure the information content of a human genome, but
>what's the information content of, for example, an enzyme?   

Excellent point.  The genome encodes the primary structure (the sequence of
amino-acids) of a protein explicitly, and can be thought to determine the
secondary structure (the sheet or helix created (at the ribosome?)).  However,
the precise folding of a protein (tertiary structure) and the construction of a
complete functional enzyme (quaternary structure) are determined by the
presence of various environmental factors...  As to how the enzyme finally
acts - that is a property of a larger system, something only implicitly coded
by the genome and extremely sensitive to the environment.

>"Plan" and "motivation" are just a shorthand for the tendency of evolution 
>to select for purposive-looking structures and behaviors, namely those 
>best at propagating the metabolism-encoding genes.

Actually, what I was referring to was the notion of intentional behavior, which
to some is fundamentally different from a reactionary behavior.  I agree with
you that plan and motivation should be thought of as shorthand for an
evolutionary tendency for purposive-looking structures and behaviors...
However, I'm also intrigued by some work on intentional systems, particularly
by the ecological psychologists (Shaw, Turvey...).

>The very non-linear
>embryological process has been abstractly modelled by 
>Aristid Lindemayer's L-systems.  Very good versions of cell
>differentiation, tree branching patterns, etc. can be produced
>by L-systems.

Sounds interesting.  Got some references?

Derek Zahn writes:

>It's easy to see why the 'function' of
>the liver can be seen that way, where the equilibrium state is "alive"
>(or, more specifically, certain concentrations of substances in the blood),
>but it's not clear to me what this means when we include the environment.
>What is the equilibrium state being maintained by my diligent li'l
>neurons in the system "me + my environment"?

The equilibrium state being maintained is still "being alive", that is the
`function' of the organism as a whole.  The nervous system is just part of this
(the term is "autopoietic system") that extends the homeostasis into the
external environment.  At a lower level of abstraction one can think of the
nervous system as maintaining a consensus between perception and expectation.
This `expectation' does not need to be an explicit model of the environment,
but rather any abstraction that can be compared to some perceptual state.
Mismatch between these causes a peturbation to the system which results in a
behavior that re-establishes the consensus (action is the control of
perception).  Those perceptual abstractions and resultant behaviors that also
receive positive evolutionary feedback will be selected for and observed in
more advanced creatures such as ourselves.

But Derek asks the important question: `How does this notion give us anything
better than the computer/information theory metaphor?'.  Well, I'd argue that
it provides a framework within which to pursue the problem of cognition.
The consideration of the brain as an input/output device contributes to the
study of perception and the study of action, but it does not synthesize them
both into a functional framework.  The progression into "higher" functions
always becomes more muddled, perhaps hopelessly so.  I believe this is because
researchers tend to break down the problem of intelligent behavior along what
may be inappropriate lines - studying perceptual systems in isolation from
motor systems, etc. - losing the big picture.  What I am searching for is a
viewpoint that allows one to attack the problem as a whole, progressing from
high abstract levels to actual mechanisms, or even better, from simpler
creatures toward more complex ones.  (This is difficult within the current
academic system, as one is expected to specialize in an established field...)

>> The study of the mind should not limit itself to any particular viewpoint.
>> Computer science, information theory, mathematics, psychology, psychophysics,
>> neurobiology, neurophysiology, evolutionary theory, embryology, ethology, and
>> philosophy are each inadequate.  We can only hope that their union is enough
>
>Agreed, but again -- enough for what?
>

Enough for a sufficient understanding of the mind to allow for the construction
of intelligent entities, modification to human abilities, etc.

-Paul


