This isn't the point.  The point is not what could be remembered, but
what passes into long term storage.  I know from experience, which I
suspect we share, that when I read a scientific article I store many
things.  I remember the conclusions (ie how the article reshapes or
supports my world-view).  For a while after I can recall other stuff
like the author, the publication, dates etc with certainty.  All that
fades over a preiod of time until I'm left with not much more than the
conclusions, after a few months, and a vague feeling of where I read it.

>  What font was the text of the test in?
> How was the text arranged on the page?  Was there a blob of gunk of
> the page?  What shape was it?  What color was the
> page/text/room/chair?

These are all things that the subject isn't interested in.  Within month
or two I'd be surpised if many could recall much of these features.

> What was the subject thinking about?  What could be seen out the
> window?

Probably the subject could recall some vague idea of what she could see
out of the window because that was more interesting than the test.  But
I still doubt whether the long term bits into memory is more than double
the rate Launder quotes.

> Maybe the environment was so severely restricted that some questions
> of this type are not relevant, but then it's hardly fair to generalize
> to normal situations!

Perhaps, but my experience of normal situations suggests that this all
to likely to be true - alas! :-(

