Date: Mon, 7 Jun 93 20:26:36 EDT
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: TALK REPORT: Seth Lloyd @ MIT

I went to the talk by Seth Lloyd (of Los Alamos National Labs) at MIT today.
Cool ideas.  Here's the gist:

Immagine a row of atoms whose states (by some magic) don't interact at all.
The electron shells of atoms have states that you can knock them between
with light pulses of the right characteristics.  Given atoms in a certain
state, you can knock them all into a certain other state with a certain
pulse.  (The same pulse again has the reverse effect--I don't know whether
this is generally true or you have to set it up so it's true, but I got
the impression that it was generally true.)  So,

Trick one: You can flip the set of atoms in one of two states into the
opposite of the two, leaving atoms in other states alone.  He says the
ability to make pulses of the right characteristics has already been
demonstrated.  The pulses are from 10 femtosecond to a picosecond long.

Next, imagine weak coupling between neighboring atoms only.  This is hard
to set up, but when you have it, the state of a given atom is nudged up
or down in energy based on the states of its neighbors.  This nudging
of the state effects what frequency of light it will react to.  So,

Trick two: You can flip those atoms that are in a certain state whose
neighbors are in a certain state.

With this trick, you have everything you need to make a finite state
automaton.  Only this is the very useful kind of FSA that lets you
change the rules at every step.  Lloyd has done the basics of figuring
out how to take a given logic circuit and compile it into a sequence
of pulses.  The length of the sequence is basically the total length
of the wires in the circuit, since moving bits across the array is
the thing that takes time.

Translation: even without any very quantumy things going on, this is
a very general and powerful single-instruction-stream, multiple-data 
(SIMD) computing model.

The operations are reversable: to undo a computation, you just
run the same set of pulses in the opposite order.

There are all sorts of conditions that have to be taken care of, and
effects that have to be compensated for (spontaneous decays, longer-
range interactions, etc.) and he's not sure whether it will all work out, 
but the math and physics he's learned and done so far says okay.  One thing
he did calculate was a rough estimate of how much redundancy you'd need
for very large calculations.  He said with
	0.5 % error rate
	10^15 bits of state
	a 10^20 step computation
you'd need 50 times redundancy (using a simple voting scheme).  He said
this was probably optimistic since his error calculation assumes that 
the chances of errors are independent when they're not.  Error correction
works by pushing erroneous atoms into short-lived states that emit
photons.  That way you can get atoms in two different states to end up
in the same state.  That's also a method of getting output out of the
array.  You could also use the standard Fredkin gate tricks and pump
the noise out the sides.

(By the way, he said 10^15 bits over 10^20 steps was probably more
calculation than you'd ever need, and I don't know if it was a joke
but I suppressed laughter (on the impolite order of "Hah, hah, hah,
what do you mortals know of computation!").  After all, modern 64 bit 
computers can address more than 10^22 bits, and a mole is around 10^24.)

The third trick was, with slightly different pulses, you can put the
atoms (and therefore the computer) in superpositions of states.  There was 
not much discussion of what to do after that as far as computing 
applications (no one's certain whether it buys you much), but truly
tamper-proof/tamper-detecting computers were mentioned in passing.
Keeping the computer in a smeary state is hard since any time you do
get a spontaneous emmision (those 0.5% errors) the system gets into
a more squeezed state.

He said the main use of the quantum version would probably be for
physics experiments.  For instance, there's a version of the Bell
inequality for three (instead of two) correllated thingys that shows even
more striking paradoxes, and this system can generate that kind of state
easily.

Another potential application would be setting up states of, or
giving instructions to, masses of nanomachines in parallel.

The beginning of the talk was a summary of the history of physics of
computing ideas.  One point he made was about a trick physicists do 
called "diagonalizing" the matrix that represents a system.  This puts
it in a form where you can easily calculate the final state for any
given starting state and amount of time.  He said even if you had a
perfect theory, putting the (huge) matrix in this form is as difficult
as doing all the possible computations the system could do!

To get on the mailing list for this new series of Physics-Of-Computation
talks, I'm not sure whether you should write to
	poc@im.lcs.mit.edu
or	poc-request@im.lcs.mit.edu
As Robin says, you might want to get on it just for the abstracts.	

Oh, yes.  He mentioned that Santa Fe (near Los Alamos) seems to be the
crystal healing capital of the world, and now he can go around saying
that he's working on getting intelligent crystals to solve problems by
shining colored lights at them.  He met a guy who purges bad vibrations
from crystals and makes more money than he does.

