Date: Mon, 7 Jun 1993 19:34:27 -0700 (PDT)
From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: MEME: What is it?

On Mon, 7 Jun 1993, Derek Zahn wrote:

> Can anybody give me a good definition of a 'meme'?

I very much doubt it.

> I sort of use
> it for any "transmissable" mental pattern, but that seems more fuzzy
> than I'd like.  I've read _The Selfish Gene_.

where it is defined as (something like) "unit of imitation", which is pretty
much the same as "'transmissible' mental pattern", which is indeed pretty 
fuzzy.  But to unfuzz it you need some things which you haven't got.  (Or
if you have, your thesis worries are over).
     An analagous definition for _gene_ would be something like "transmissible
physical pattern", which is likewise pretty fuzzy.  For genes we can do 
better, coming from either of two directions.  There's the Mendelian take,
where we identify genes with traits, and do the segregation studies to 
determine which traits or bundles of traits transmit as units.  And there's
the molecular definition, where we can identify coding units, and then
find sequences of coding units which transmit as units.  There's still
room to argue about what a "gene" is, but the argument is only about which
of various well-understood levels of unit the term is most usefully applied
to.
    Well, to do the equivalent for _meme_, you need to be able to do at
least one of the analagous tasks--identify the atomic units of transmitted
behavior or "mental patterns", or break the code in which these patterns
are stored.  The latter, of course, entails first figuring out how 
information is stored at the neuron level--there was just some discussion
of that issue.  IMO we're even farther away from the former--every now
and then somebody, without thinking the problem through very far, will
suggest "words" or "concepts" for the analogue to traits, but those aren't
even close.



