  Don Davis writes:

Thats Dan, by the way.

  >Note also that if we allow white paper logic, we can also say that
  >a white piece of paper provides evidence that crows are white.

  That does not work. It does increase your belief that all things smaller 
  than a buick are white. 

And if you know that all crows are smaller than a buick...

  But it does not follow that it increases your 
  belief that crows are white. 

  In order for all things smaller than a 
  buick to be white, it is necessary that all (not enormous) pieces of 
  paper be white, that all crows be white, that ...  . You have raised 
  the joint probability by raising the probability of one of the 
  necessary conditions. That tells us nothing about the other conditions. 

I have just used your principle: if I see an X, and it is Blah, that
provides evidence that all Xs are Blah. (call this principle *)

X - Smaller than a buick
Blah - White

You reply, if I understand you, that the white piece of paper only 
provides evidence that all non enormous pieces of paper are white.

You seem to be missing my point. I might agree with the actual result
here, that the white piece of paper actually only provides evidence
that all pieces of paper are white. I would agree with it, because
I don't think using deduction on arbitrary sets makes for good induction.

Your "contrapositive crows" paper, however, uses extremely arbitrary
sets, Not(A) type of sets, and your original proposition * supposedly
holds for any set. If you want to make the claim that all things
smaller than a buick does not make for a good set upon which to
apply your principle, then fine, I agree. That is in fact my point.
You cannot use your heuristic on arbitrary sets, whether arrived
at by deductive manipulation, or pulled out of the air, like "things smaller
than a buick." Thus "contrapositive crows" is an invalid argument based on
invalid methodology. If you think it uses valid methodology, and in
particular, that your evidence heuristic holds for any set, then you have to
accept my buick argument. It uses exactly those principles. If you accept
the buick argument, and your methodology, you have to conclude that our
belief that crows are black should go both up and down upon the same
evidence. If your reasoning leads you to such an absurdity, the reasoning 
should be thrown out.

