Another hodge-podge post, this one consisting of one 
request for information, ramblings about several sf books, 
comments on vegan/rec.food.veg and cutting down sleep time.
Since these seem to be the only style of post I make to this
list, I thought I'd add a prefix.  If MISC would be more appropriate,
I'll use that in future, instead.

Oh, and an au revoir to Mr. Morrow.  I, for one, will be awaiting
your return.

The June SciAm had an interesting article entitled "Eugenics
Revisited", regarding recent work in behavioral genetics (a field
in which I must confess to being woefully ignorant).  The claim
is made that certain researchers want to demonstrate that certain
behavioral traits (tendency to alcholism, schizophrenia, sexual
orientation among them) are, if not genetically determined, at least
genetically influenced, in hopes that this will spawn more tolerance.
(An argument that, in retrospect, strikes me as very similar to
what Kaminer rails against in the book I mention later in this post,
that is, argument from disease/victimhood  -- it's ok because it's
not my fault.  Bizarre, and something that, once adopted, I suspect
leads to a lack of inclination to attempt to fix the problem.  This
is the fundamental problem I have with all those studies which
attempt to show certain abilities are sex linked/influenced.  I
honestly don't *care* which sex is more apt on average with math.
I think everyone should try to do as well as possible and excuses
like the ones such studies would provide would tend to work against
such an effort, on account of fatalistic tendencies in many humans.)
I am inclined to believe, as the author of the article seems to,
that this is really very optimistic, and the actual results in the
political arena could range from selective abortion to compulsive 
sterilization to much, much worse (statism being what it is).  
I noticed a book entitled _Exploding the Gene Myth_ by Ruth Hubbard 
and Elijah Ward, which seemed to cover a lot of similar ground (spotted 
this hc on the science shelves at a bookstore).  My first inclination is to
say, "Yay!  Sanity reigns once again" (I'm on the nurture side of
this debate, for the most part); my second is to say, "Oh, gee,
guess the pendulum's gonna head the other way for a while".  My
request, then, is:  Is there anyone lurking on this list who
is well-informed on the topic?  Is the SciAm article a fair
overview of recent work?  Is the Hubbard/Ward book worth $24,
or could someone suggest a more worthy book for my money? 

I finally got around to reading Melinda Snodgrass' Circuit
Trilogy, which was mentioned on this list back when I first
started reading (in the middle of a very long discussion
about extropian books/fictions/etc.).  Near future legal sf,
the tale of a political hack who is sent to impose law 'n
order on the independent minded stations and bases of the System
(who previously had a justice system based on voluntary arbitration).
Hack wises up and turns the tables on his Earthside bosses (totalitarian
scum, one and all), and goes from the moon (_Circuit_) to
Mars (_Circuit Breaker_ -- odd Mormon colony that's trying to
terraform Mars by dumping an asteroid or three onto the
polar ice caps in an effort to free up the water, etc.) to
the asteroids (mining colonies, of course, with a base on Ceres).
Lots of references to key players in the American Revolution.
I'd have liked these books a lot more, only pretty much all of
the characters (but especially the women) made the hair raise on
the back of my neck.  I do not think they were intended to be
unlikable, but they came across that way to me.  Which makes
this a sort of recommendation on account of the ideas the books
depict, but not really, on account of the style wasn't fab, and
the characters were grating and somewhat stereotypical.  On the
other hand, they are short (I read them in one day).

In fact, I read another (very short) book on the same day:  _Fup_
by Jim Dodge, a California author.  Dodge also wrote _Stone Junction_,
a book which uses some of the same conspiracy stuff that shows up
in Illuminatus and Foucault's Pendulum, only the actual organization
isn't -- just a group of people who have similar goals, the leaders
have no coercive power, but if they don't like what you're doing, they
won't help you.  Won't necessarily hurt you, either.   Self-described
anarchists and alchemists -- a lot of the stuff relating to the latter
may grate with extropians more than the rest of the book makes up for.
The style and techniques the group is depicted as using, however,
are fascinating.  Lots of mind games.  Fairly subversive.  The
group does not use violence -- no one is allowed to kill, except
with their imagination (the explanation of this is a riot, and
highly reminiscent of Vinge's "The Ungoverned", and the bluffs
used against the tuvela in James Schmitz's novel _The Demon Breed_,
but those are, of course, other stories).  _Fup_ is much less 
ambitious, describing only a couple of characters, one of whom 
succeeds in ignoring the government for about 50 years
(as in, doesn't pay taxes).  Doesn't really recognize their existence until
he wants something from them.  :-)

I just finished reading a book called _I'm Dysfunctional, You're
Dysfunctional_, by Wendy Kaminer.  While not extropian (not even
I can stretch the definition that far), the book is entertaining
and, imo, worth reading.  She discusses recovery groups (esp.
12 step programs), not from the point of view of effectiveness,
but rather critiques their popularity (for everything from
sex addiction to shopaholics) from a political point of view:
she argues that 12 step programs in general are dangerous in
a democracy, and constitute a trend towards authoritarianism.
The book also has some on-target polemic on other "self-help"
programs:  Erhard's est and the current version, The Forum, which
I recall being mentioned here a few months back, and was really
the reason I bothered to mention this book here at all; positive
thinking a la Norman Vincent Peale, etc.  Her complaint about this
other extreme is that these approaches fail to take into consideration
circumstance and chance, postulating that everything is the result
of one's character/attitude, hence compassion for the poor/neurotic/etc.
is unwarranted and useless.  By comparison, the worst depiction
of anarchocapitalism/extropianism is more charitable.  ;)  Vintage
published the paperback, I believe.  It seems to be stuck in the
psychology section (altho it doesn't really belong there, imo).
She draws somewhat on James' _Varieties of Religious Experience_ in
discussing the historical antecedents of some of these movements.

I started reading rec.food.veg in search of vegan recipes a while
back when I started spending a lot of time cooking (I figure it's
a bad sign when going out to eat is depressing, and about time to
try something else).  I have severe allergies to milk products
and my husband has a long list of allergies, including beef, poultry,
and eggs.  Vegan recipes seemed like the way to go.  I've been
pleasantly surprised to find out how many baked goods can be prepared
without eggs or milk, and without detrimental effect on taste
(since my non-vegan, milk drinking, egg eating friends like them, I
conclude that this is not merely a case of a twisted palate).

I was a little surprised at the complaint about baked bean dishes
being so time consuming.  I haven't made any bean dishes per se,
but I have made several bean based soups; they are no more time
consuming than anything else.  You just have to plan menus a day
or more ahead.  *shrug*  Maybe I misunderstood.

The real time killer, imo, is fresh vegetables.  I've really got
to get faster at chopping.  :-)  I haven't bothered to buy a wok,
yet, because I wasn't sure how much I'd like stir fry; I use
some castiron, ceramic coated pans (I grew up using aluminum and
never really understood the concept of frypan as weapon until
I got these pans) and very, very, very little oil.   I use
wine, instead, and a lid -- the effect is somewhere between
steaming and stir-frying.  Tasty.  Not being up on the subject,
I won't speculate about carcinogens, etc.  It's undoubtedly
better for me than a hamburger.

Stanton McCandlish <anton@hydra.unm.edu> writes:

>The time issue reminds me: Is anything being done to reduce or elminate SLEEP?
>It irks me mightly to spend 1/3 of my time sleeping.  I have thought of trying
>to gradually reduce the amount of time I spend sleeping so that it is down
>from 8 to 4 or so hours (perhaps over 6 months or so).  SO, has anyone on
>the list tried this? Does it work? Do you lose any effectiveness or
>concentration or energy? 

Well, for some reason, as yet inexplicable to me, I didn't start pulling
frequent all-nighters until very late in college.  But when I did,
I was amazed to find out how easy it was.  Afterwards, I never
did get back to my old 8 hours -- it tends to stay down around 5 or
6, jumping up to about 10 when I get sick (which doesn't happen 
any more often than before I started shorting myself on sleep).   In
my case, it turns out that a few hours of sleep well after midnight
are worth a whole lot more than any amount of sleep before midnight,
flying directly in the face of a whole slew of claims my mother
used to nag me with.  I would suggest just trying cutting off an
hour of your sleeptime, and see what happens.  The only significant
effect I've seen is that I stay slightly more, uh, hyper than I
used to be.  Concentration is about the same as before (which is
to say, comes and goes), but the extra time translated into riding
a few new hobbies, which is capable of distracting me from things
I probably ought to be doing.  :-)

